\section{Introduction to Dataflow Analysis}\label{sec:dataflow-intro}

Dataflow analysis is one of the cornerstones of modern compiler technology. It provides a systematic, mathematically rigorous framework for gathering information about how values flow through a program---information that is indispensable for optimization, verification, and program understanding. Unlike program execution, which follows a single path determined by the input, dataflow analysis considers \emph{all} possible execution paths simultaneously, producing a conservative approximation of what might happen at each program point.

The origins of dataflow analysis trace back to the early 1970s, when researchers such as Kildall~\cite{kildall1973unified} and Kam, Ullman, and Tarjan~\cite{kam1976new} recognized that many seemingly different program analyses could be unified under a common algebraic framework. This insight transformed compiler design: rather than crafting bespoke algorithms for each analysis, compiler writers could instantiate a general framework with appropriate parameters.

In this chapter, we develop the theoretical foundations of dataflow analysis. We begin by defining the control-flow graph, the structural substrate upon which dataflow analyses operate. We then introduce dataflow facts, transfer functions, and the combination rules that govern how information propagates through a program. The central results---the meet-over-all-paths solution and the maximal fixedpoint---are developed in detail, and we prove their relationship. Finally, we present the worklist algorithm, the standard practical method for computing dataflow solutions, and establish its correctness and complexity.

\section{The Control-Flow Graph}\label{sec:cfg}

Before we can analyze the flow of data through a program, we need a representation of the program's control structure. The \emph{control-flow graph} (CFG) serves this purpose.

\begin{definition}[Control-Flow Graph]\label{def:cfg}
A \textbf{control-flow graph} is a directed graph $G = (N, E, n_0, n_\mathit{exit})$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $N$ is a finite set of \textbf{nodes} (also called \textbf{basic blocks}),
  \item $E \subseteq N \times N$ is a set of directed \textbf{edges} representing possible transfers of control,
  \item $n_0 \in N$ is the unique \textbf{entry node},
  \item $n_\mathit{exit} \in N$ is the unique \textbf{exit node}.
\end{itemize}
Every node in $N$ is reachable from $n_0$, and $n_\mathit{exit}$ is reachable from every node in $N$.
\end{definition}

Each node $n \in N$ represents a \emph{basic block}: a maximal sequence of consecutive instructions in which control flow enters at the beginning and leaves at the end, without any halt, branch, or jump except at the entry and exit.

\begin{definition}[Basic Block]\label{def:basic-block}
A \textbf{basic block} is a maximal sequence of instructions $s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_k$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Control can only enter the block through the first instruction $s_1$.
  \item Control can only leave the block through the last instruction $s_k$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The construction of basic blocks from a sequence of instructions is a standard compiler phase. The algorithm identifies \emph{leaders}---instructions that begin a new block---and then groups instructions between consecutive leaders.

\begin{definition}[Predecessors and Successors]\label{def:pred-succ}
For a node $n$ in a CFG, we define:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathit{pred}(n) = \{m \mid (m, n) \in E\}$, the set of \textbf{predecessors} of $n$,
  \item $\mathit{succ}(n) = \{m \mid (n, m) \in E\}$, the set of \textbf{successors} of $n$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

